From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tom Englund <tomenglund26@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 18:25:01 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] nvidia-drm: add the scaling mode connector property

KWin, like other compositors, offers common lower resolutions on laptop
panels (1920x1200, 1600x1200, ...) only when the connector has the
standard "scaling mode" property with "Full aspect", and then uses it for
those modes. nvidia-drm didn't have it, so the internal panel could only
run at its native resolution. Laptop panels often accept nothing but
their own timings (this one's EDID allows a single 432 kHz line rate), so
the driver has to drive the native timings and scale the smaller image
with the display hardware, as i915 and amdgpu do.

NVKMS can already do that: a modeset request takes the size the head
fetches (viewPortSizeIn) and the rectangle within the raster it scales
it to (viewPortOut). KAPI always set the input size to the mode's visible
size and never passed an output rectangle. Add both to the KAPI head
modeset config, used when the new viewPort.enabled is set.

nvidia-drm attaches "scaling mode" (None, Full, Center, Full aspect) to
every connector, None by default, which drives the mode as requested,
like before. get_modes saves the modes at the display's native
resolution (the preferred mode's size). A new encoder atomic_check, used
when a modeset is needed and the CRTC drives a single connector, takes
the native mode with the closest refresh rate as the adjusted mode when
scaling is requested and the mode is smaller than the native resolution,
and computes the viewport: the whole raster (Full), the mode's size
centered (Center), or the largest centered rectangle with the mode's
aspect ratio (Full aspect). The CRTC's mode keeps the requested size,
which the planes are positioned in. The CRTC check now passes the
adjusted mode to KAPI, so the vblank timing, which already used it,
matches what is driven. A change of the property forces a modeset.

nvidia_drm.scaling_mode=0 leaves the property out.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
